# Final semantic scorecard to present

Show the output beginning with `A. ALIAS VERIFICATION`, or show the saved final comparison report.

Say:

> After the contract audit, I ran one final semantic check over the same 36 stored runs. Five alias mappings were accepted only after their values matched the expected result row by row. Two booking-percentage aliases were rejected because they matched only 17 of 20 rows.
>
> The final business result is this:
>
> The Wide Baseline answered 7 of 18 runs correctly, which is 38.9 percent.
>
> The Booking Scope view answered 18 of 18 correctly, which is 100 percent for these six tested booking patterns.
>
> That is an observed improvement of 61.1 percentage points.

Then explain consistency:

> The Booking Scope view was stable for all six question groups. The Wide Baseline was stable for four, unstable for the lowest-fulfillment question, and one above-CSAL group could not be evaluated consistently because the booking-percentage alias did not produce the same values.

Then explain safety:

> All 36 runs stayed within their assigned source. There were no cross-table references and no SELECT-star queries. Both agents also refused the unsupported swap request.

Then explain the honest limitation:

> Correctness improved, but speed did not. The Wide Baseline median response time was 18.1 seconds. The Booking Scope median was 24.3 seconds.
>
> SQL complexity was mixed too. The focused view produced more accurate answers, but its generated SQL was not smaller on every measure. So the proven benefit here is correctness and consistency, not latency.

