# Likely manager questions and short answers

## Why not just let Genie use the wide Gold table?

> We can keep the wide table for the pipeline and existing consumers. The issue for Genie is that several business grains and similar metrics are exposed together. A focused view gives Genie a clearer contract for one type of question.

## Is the data in the wide table wrong?

> No. This benchmark does not prove bad source data. It shows that Genie interpreted the wide contract less reliably.

## Why did you build facts and dimensions if Genie queries only the view?

> The facts separate each measure by the grain where it belongs, which makes validation safer. The view then hides that complexity and gives Genie one simple interface.

## Does 100 percent mean it can answer every CSM question?

> No. It means 18 of 18 runs were correct for six verified booking-scope patterns. Other domains still need their own tests.

## Was production changed?

> No. Production stayed read-only and unchanged. All new objects and Genie spaces were isolated POC assets.

## Why was the focused view slower?

> We observed the difference, but this pilot did not isolate the cause. A separate performance test is needed before attributing it to the view, warehouse, cache, or query expansion.

## Were both agents configured exactly the same?

> They used the same warehouse, questions, and intended instruction contract. The API did not expose every setting for independent verification, so I documented that limitation.

## Why did the reported score change during the audit?

> The first scoring contract expected extra columns for V5 and mixed naming compliance with business correctness. I corrected the scoring against the original stored runs. I did not ask the agents new questions.

## Are the cancellation and rejection thresholds now data-driven?

> No. V1 keeps the existing stored flags exactly as they are. Historical threshold calibration is separate work.

## Is the swap recommender ready?

> No. This V1 deliberately refuses executable swap requests. Swap scoring needs approved matching rules, reliable point-in-time history, outcome labels, and a separate controlled validation.

## What should happen next?

> Review this Booking Scope evidence first. If the approach is accepted, choose one additional domain, define its grain and verified business questions, build one narrow view, and repeat the same benchmark.

