# Things not to claim

- Do not say the wide table is bad just because it has many columns.
- Do not say the wide table contains incorrect data.
- Do not say production was optimized or changed.
- Do not say the personal schema is private.
- Do not say every Gold column was validated.
- Do not say the full fact-and-dimension architecture is proven.
- Do not say the Booking Scope view is faster.
- Do not say its SQL is always simpler.
- Do not describe the stored risk flags as probabilities.
- Do not say the current thresholds were historically calibrated.
- Do not say swap recommendations or reservable capacity are ready.
- Do not quote the earlier `16.7% vs 83.3%` or `16.7% vs 94.4%` results as the final business score.

## Safe wording

> For the six booking-scope patterns tested, the focused semantic view improved Genie correctness and repeatability. Production remained unchanged, latency did not improve, and other CSM and CSAL domains still require separate validation.
